  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Total federal contract obligations to Hawaii are $2.4B in FY2026 to date. The state has 22,166 awards so far, with an average award size of $110K, and obligations fell 55.9% year over year. Yearly history shows obligations rose from $2.8B in FY21 to $6.7B in FY24, then fell to $5.5B in FY25 and to $2.4B in FY26. The Department of Defense is the largest buying agency in FY2026 to date, obligating $1.9B. Other significant buyers include the General Services Administration at $300M and the Department of Homeland Security at $118M. Construction and engineering-related industries lead spending, with Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $603M and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $466M. Major contractors include DRAGADOS/HAWAIIAN DREDGING/ORION JV at $355M and NAN INC at $272M. This snapshot covers fiscal year to date obligations and reflects partial current-year totals.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies are awarding the most federal contracts in Hawaii?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the top buyer, obligating $1.9B in FY2026 to date. The General Services Administration follows with $300M, and the Department of Homeland Security follows with $118M. Several civilian agencies also appear among the top buyers, including the Department of Veterans Affairs at $46.2M and the Department of the Interior at $36.1M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which vendors and industries capture the largest shares of federal spending in Hawaii?",
        "body": "DRAGADOS/HAWAIIAN DREDGING/ORION JV is the largest vendor at $355M, and NAN INC is the second largest at $272M. Other top vendors include CONTI FEDERAL SERVICES, LLC at $97.3M and HENSEL PHELPS CONSTRUCTION CO at $89.5M. On the industry side, Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction leads at $603M, followed by Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $466M and Engineering Services at $289M."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much federal contract money goes to Hawaii?",
      "a": "Total federal contract obligations to Hawaii are $2.4B in FY2026 to date. The state has 22,166 awards so far, with an average award size of $110K, and obligations fell 55.9% year over year. Historical totals by fiscal year were $2.8B in FY21, $3.5B in FY22, $4.4B in FY23, $6.7B in FY24, and $5.5B in FY25."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which agencies award the most contracts in Hawaii?",
      "a": "The Department of Defense awards the most contracts in Hawaii, obligating $1.9B in FY2026 to date. The General Services Administration is next at $300M, followed by the Department of Homeland Security at $118M. Other agencies in the top ten include the Department of Veterans Affairs at $46.2M and the Department of the Interior at $36.1M."
